{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1225787-dallas-dfw-texas-changeling-the-lost-beginners-take","id":1225787,"title":"Dallas DFW Texas Changeling The Lost, beginners take.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-06-07T18:43:35-07:00","id":1225787,"title":"Dallas DFW Texas Changeling The Lost, beginners take.","content":"I am pretty new to this but after reading a few different books and watching a group play a chronicle online I decided I would try to start a source post for Dallas Texas in the Changeling universe. I hope to incorporate Vampire and other games in the same Chronicles of Darkness, I just have to read up on The Requiem, The Forsaken, and probably The Awakened first. What I'm posting below is my proposed Dallas timeline to the present and generally suggested alternate history of how Dallas came to be alongside their freehold growth. There are probably comma errors. This is basically me asking if I'm working in the right direction or if I need to rethink my approach. This is very much first draft but yes, I just want to hear back from others who are more experienced with this to let me know if I'm on the right track or I'm fundamentally overlooking anything. Any feedback is appreciated! <br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">PRE-SETTLEMENT HISTORY: Though many tribes inhabited the areas surrounding where the Dallas-Fort Worth Metroplex would be, the Caddo peoples were a prominent tribe that held territory where current day Dallas is. Changeling scholars speculate over the Caddo Tribe creation story as a potential allegory for the Hedge. The story goes that the tribe was led from a cave called </span></span><span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>Chahkanina</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> or &quot;the place of crying,&quot; by their leader Moon who instructed them to not look back as they left. When the people and animals who made up the tribe were out, Wolf looked back and the cave sealed up trapping those still inside. The ancestors of the tribe then instructed the people various religious ceremonies as well as practical skills. While impossible to determine if this was a mass migration from the Hedge or not is impossible to say. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Eastern Cross Timbers in Denton area had numerous entrances to the Hedge as unwittingly pointed out by Athanase de Mezieres, a French explorer in the service of Spain in 1778. \u201c</span></span><span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">the natives appropriately call [it] the Grand Forest. ...it is very dense, but not very wide. It seems to be there as a guide to even the most inexperienced, and to give refuge in this dangerous region to those who, few in number and lacking in courage, wish to go from one village to another.\u201d </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Changelings speculate a system of entrances once existed for the natives, but with the Indian Removal Act of 1830, Changeling Natives destroyed most gateways behind them. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">SETTLEMENT OF DALLAS : In 1855 after the failed attempt of a group of French, Belgian, and Swiss utopian experiment \u201cLa R\u00e9union\u201d the remaining artists and skilled colonist were absorbed into the Dallas community where their influence can still be felt today in neighborhoods like Deep Ellum. With them came a handful of Fairest and Wizened Changelings. Already present in Dallas was a scattering of Beasts and Elementals among the settlers helping build the city. On Feb. 2nd, 1856, with a population of 678, Dallas established its charter. On the same evening, numbering 22 in total, the first freehold of Dallas was established. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">GROWTH AND EXPANSION: The Changeling population grew steadily as the population of Dallas shot to over 7000 by 1872 with the establishment of railroads intersecting in Dallas making it a commercial hub and last stop for anyone traveling west. As the city grew and annexed more and more communities into itself, a network of Changeling authority had to be established. The various motleys and courtless persona of East Dallas and Oakcliff did not respond kindly to a proposed contract with the \u201cproper\u201d Dallas elite Changelings who wanted to impose a poll tax. The infighting of the changeling groups grew so intense the entire population was caught off guard by the arrival of a True Fae on May 25th in 1908 with an entourage of huntsmen set on recapturing as many Changelings as they could. The arrival of the True Fae brought with her a storm so intense that the next day the Trinity River flooded, displacing 5,000 people and destroying countless homes. The Changeling population suffered 20 losses, hoping they were drowned but presumed captured, bringing the population down to 42. Shaken by their loss, the disparate courts and motleys regrouped to construct the greater Contract of Unity of 1908.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">DEPRESSION &amp; WW2: The steady economic growth of Dallas at the beginning of the century was not totally halted by the onset of the great depression as the 1930 oil boom in nearby Kilgore. As Dallas became the oil capital of Texas at the time, a strange new type of Changeling emerged in the community; Crudebloods. When </span></span><span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Columbus Marion &quot;Dad&quot; Joiner struck oil, he also fractured a deep underground portal in the hedge allowing for strange elemental Changelings with oil running through their veins to escape. The Changeling population boomed as Crudebloods, alongside other Elementals, Darklings, and various Wizened poured into the Dallas freehold doubling the population to 122. All seasonal courts were in high spirits. Spring and Autumn were able to feed off the greed and envy of the rich and poor alike, while Summer and Winter fed off the mixed emotions of the impending war to come. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">PRESENT DAY DALLAS CHANGELING SOCIETY (MINUS PARTICULAR PLOT SQUABBLES): As Dallas grew into the economic power that it is now, so too did the Changeling Freehold of Dallas rise in prominence. Freeholds in other cities know the Dallas courts excels in ways few other freeholds do. The Autumn court boasts a Changeling college, hosts inter-freehold conferences, and collaborates with other Autumn courts to share new research. Dallas Summer court Militia often serve as hired guards and mercenaries when other Freeholds cannot tackle a specific problem. A motley by the name of Red Dirt has considerable notoriety in this regard. Dallas Winter court specializes in the growing tech industry of Dallas, focusing on surveillance and alarm systems. The unique rivalry between Devout Spring and Joyful Spring has cultivated a court of intensity. Surely, other courts try to one-up each other, but Dallas spring is in a competition with </span></span><span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>itself</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> with rivals each trying to put on an even more grandiose display of their mantle and emotion. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">While a separate entity, the freehold of Fort Worth works in tandem with its sister freehold in Dallas. This has two leaders for each court coming together to rule over their respective seasons and make decisions for Metroplex Changeling population now numbering 173.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">THE FOUNDING OF THE COURTS: Previous to the Contract of Unity, Changelings operated on a tight close-knit regional system that could not support a fully developed court system divided into four seasons. Essentially, there was the Camp of Frontier, and the Urban Camp, the former slowly absorbed by the later. While various contracts still exist cultivated by the Camp of Frontier, they are considered anachronistic and quaint at best, defunct and ill-suited more often than not in contemporary Changeling society as the Metroplex grows ever more saturated with Urban sprawl. Following the flood of the Trinity in 1908 - and the regional contract that followed - four seasonal courts were established, partly in alignment for a desire for the community to function in a way that made it stronger, but also partly as a desire to show off to other freeholds that Dallas was a </span></span><span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>contemporary </i></span></span><span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Changeling city with a fully functioning court system. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The first two courts established were Summer and Spring. Capitan Jeremiah Hutchins, aka Red Jerry, was a transplant from the budding Fort Worth freehold. A Capitan in the US army sent to evacuate Fort Worth in 1853, he was captured near a functioning gate in the Eastern Cross Timbers. His durrance in Arcadia, one of brutish Ogre Farwalker, he escaped, but only after securing a victory for his Keepers army. He was incapable of deserting his fellow soldiers if it put them at a disadvantage. One of the few who felt the battle brewing on the horizon that fateful day in May, he rode a horse nearly to death through the various Hedge shortcuts to get from Fort Worth to Dallas to inform the bickering factions of the coming Fae just before they arrived themselves. Red marshaled the scattered changelings into a militia and managed to hold off the forces of the huntsmen and True Fae during the night and in the aftermath was elected to be the Summer Marshal. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The oddities of the primary Spring court in Dallas can trace their roots to their first leader, Miss Matilda Tithe. Miss Matilda wandered into the Hedge following what she believed to be the holy ghost. As a Fairest Bright One, she was fashioned into an adoring seraph of a vainglorious Fae who thought himself a god. Upon returning, instead of taking her durance as a curse, Miss Matilda redoubled her devotion to the church. A paragon of devotion, the relish, and rapture she experiences and could even share in her desire for closeness to god serve as an example for all who desire Springs blessings. She was desire incarnate, even if she was chaste and refrained from all worldly pleasures. Thus, the spring court grew in two different factions; one emploring the standard path of hedonism and pleasure, and one (elevated by church dollars) that insisted on piety and a desire for order in the mess that was life post-escape. Her two predecessors have taken Tithe as part of their title as well, in honor of Springs devotion to giving back what it takes from the Earth. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">A rivalry Spring court exists in Dallas. Springing up amidst the 60\u2019s counterculture, rival spring is everything \u201cDevout\u201d spring is not: indulgent, materialistic, and focused on present and pressing desires, the rival spring court takes pleasure in scandal and offense. Their founder, Jack Greenteeth, advocated free love, communal living, and freedom of expression in all its forms. Considered a healthy reality check by some and gouache or irreverent by others, rival Spring entertains a number of members who work with other courts seeking pleasure of the more unrestrained sort, be they drugs, sex, or otherwise outside the rules of the hymnal and \u201cgood book.\u201d </span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Winter court established itself under the watchful eye of Officer Nathaniel Dye. A police officer who worked the night shift, his natural talent for stealth and keen observation made him a natural pick for the position. Under his care the Winter court, while relatively smaller than the others, served as the watchdogs of the city refusing to be caught by surprise again. As technology improved, the winter court invested in surveillance technology. Now, it\u2019s a difficult thing indeed for a Changeling to slip past the ever vigilant notice of the Dallas Winter court. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Autumn had a difficult time establishing a leader for their court with such a lack of knowledgeable and competent candidates. With a lack of scholars or occultists, Autumn went all in for fear, elected a Changeling called Unkind Gretchen as their leader. A lady in white waits at the crossroads weeping just softly enough to be heard. When a kind stranger reaches out to help her, a shattered visage rises up to scare the very soul out shock the good Samaritan, then she disappears completely. For weeks afterward, the stranger thinks they keep seeing a woman in white out of the corner of their eye. Sometimes she\u2019s there but then gone after a blink. As shock became terror, and terror became dread, Unkind Gretchin thus fed off the unlucky souls who tried to help her. Modeled after their leaders tactics, the Autumn terrors of Dallas started a slow-burning fear in the cities heart. Don\u2019t walk home alone at night, don\u2019t talk to strangers after dark, and if you do, make sure you\u2019re in the light. </span></span><span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">When SMU was built in 1911, the Autumn court sought to establish a connection with the university. Now, the university unknowingly employs 3 Changeling Professors and host Changeling conferences once a year after hours. </span></span><br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Only in a world of ugliness is beauty a crime, a sin to find loveliness within one self.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24113-woodblood","name":"Woodblood","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24113_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-24T10:44:58-07:00","id":1229822,"title":"","content":"Dallas native.<br /> <br /> I like the idea of the idea of the rival Spring Courts.<br /> <br /> If I were to run a Changeling game set in Dallas, I would set it in the mid-80s, when the city went from boom to bust overnight and the height of the culture clash between fundamentalists and liberals.<br /> <br /> I could see the Summer Court deeply engaged in the WCCW at the Sportatorium and also with the Mesquite Rodeo.<br /> <br /> The Winter Court having secret meetings in the pedestrian tunnels under downtown.<br /> <br /> The Spring Court running the Renaissance Fair in Waxahachie and having bacchanals at the Starck Club<span style=\"font-family:nyt-imperial, georgia, times new roman, times, serif\"><span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-size:19px\">.</span></span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12488-jeremysbrain","name":"Jeremysbrain","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"209"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-26T11:35:16-07:00","id":1230333,"title":"","content":"I've since made some changes to my original plan of one gigantic freehold for all of Dallas. I have a writing goal next month with a writing prompt for each day. I was thinking instead splitting the DFW Changeling population up into four freeholds (Dallas, Fort Worth, Denton area, Frisco/Plano area) And each having more or less all four courts in those areas. Then the leaders of the four courts would get together to form a comity for the season. Then I could have rival Spring courts (Dallas decadence and Denton college atmosphere clashing with conservative ranchers in FW and snoody rich folk in developing north west area). Also, the areas would probably lean in a direction of a court, but not be one exclusively. Dallas for Spring just because of its urban environment for pleasure and clubbing etc. but still have a good Winter presence since it's easy to hide in a crowded Dallas street. FW I actually considered leaning Winter because it's a bunch of old cowboys who just want to be left alone lol but still having a good Summer presence as well (idk, come take our guns or something?). Denton would lean Autumn because of the two Universities there, but with the UNT and WTU you could have a lot of Spring too. North west could go any direction really. <br /> <br /> We should collaborate! <br /> I'm native Texan but only moved to DFW probably ... 8 years ago? And then I mostly stayed in Denton for college. The Winter court pedestrian tunnels idea is great! And the Ren fair idea is excellent!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Only in a world of ugliness is beauty a crime, a sin to find loveliness within one self.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24113-woodblood","name":"Woodblood","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24113_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-26T18:57:23-07:00","id":1230432,"title":"","content":"I would focus more on what kind of society each Freehold has first then decide where to place them. Although a Freehold doesn't have to be bound geographically or be exclusive to a particular territory. Here are the bones of a couple of Freehold ideas for the DFW area<br /> <br /> Technophiles - This is a freehold that embraces the future and technology. Based out of UTD and Texas Insturments, members of this freehold are young and had short durances, most being born in the last 50 years. Most members are regularly in contact with each other through social media and the internet, which they also use for extensive intelligence gathering and information dissemination. To the outside world they resemble a group of nerdy conspiracy theorists and LARPers who are constantly on the watch for &quot;monsters&quot;. <br /> <br /> The Knights of Trinity - This is a militant freehold that seeks to confront the Fae and their agents. Many members may have been or currently are law enforcement or ex-military. All members of this freehold are also members of the Texas State Guard. They often disguise their movements as Tactical Training. The Knights often meet late at night in indoor gun ranges across the Metroplex.<br /> <br /> The Antebellums - Members of this freehold are either old or had a very long durance, having been taken into Arcadia before the Civil Rights movement. The members here are all old Southern traditionalist and their views are a throwback to an age long gone. Most of the Antebellums are in denial of what they are and the world they now live in but stick together out of cultural identity. They regularly gather in the Fort Worth Stockyards.<br /> <br /> The Vagabonds - This freehold is always on the move.and mostly made up of members of the Winter Court. They are paranoid and distrustful and have a secret cant and cold drop locations for communicating.. When they meet in groups it is usually very early in the morning on a Dart Light Rail train. For unknown reasons they never leave the Dallas area though. To the outside world they look like a group of unhinged homeless people that share an unknown foreign language.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12488-jeremysbrain","name":"Jeremysbrain","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"209"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}